# SrTiO3 (Pm-3m, 5 atoms): the surfaces of the cubic perovskite
# Every surface with |h|,|k|,|l| <= 1 that is distinct under the Laue group
# m-3m: (1 1 1), (1 1 0) and (1 0 0), one termination each ->
# POSCAR_slab_SrTiO3_111_t1, _110_t1, _100_t1. (1 1 0) stacks SrTiO and O2
# planes, (1 1 1) SrO3 and Ti planes: charged planes (Tasker type III), polar.
# (1 0 0) stacks the neutral SrO and TiO2 planes: non-polar, but one face is
# TiO2 and the other SrO, so it is not symmetric.
crystod-slab -c 221_PPOSCAR_SrTiO3 --max-index 1

# The symmetric (1 0 0) slabs: pymatgen removes the layer of one face, which
# leaves TiO2 on both faces (s1, Sr2Ti3O8) or SrO on both faces (s2, Sr3Ti2O7):
# not of the SrTiO3 stoichiometry, but charge-neutral (so no charge column),
# and 7.89 A of atoms instead of 9.86 A (raise --slab-size for thicker ones).
# Symmetrized slabs are named _s1, _s2, ..., so they do not replace the _t1
# file of the first command.
crystod-slab -c 221_PPOSCAR_SrTiO3 --index 1 0 0 --symmetrize

# --no-polar: the (1 1 0) slab is polar, so it is listed as skipped and nothing
# is written
crystod-slab -c 221_PPOSCAR_SrTiO3 --index 1 1 0 --no-polar

# The first command without an input file (the POSCAR is bundled with the
# package); its three files are already there and identical, so they are kept
crystod-slab --example SrTiO3


